fix RS485 option "rx_during_tx" on kernels lacking the constant

gensio's configure only checks for the TIOCSRS485 declaration
(AC_CHECK_DECLS([TIOCSRS485], ...)), so the RS485 block in
lib/os_osops.c is compiled on every kernel that provides that ioctl
(mainline since 2.6.33).  Inside it, the "rx_during_tx" option uses
SER_RS485_RX_DURING_TX unguarded, but the constant only exists since
Linux 3.7, so kernels 2.6.33..3.6 fail to build gensio with:

  os_osops.c:3021: error: 'SER_RS485_RX_DURING_TX' undeclared

The very next option, "terminate_bus", is already wrapped in
#ifdef SER_RS485_TERMINATE_BUS - do the same for "rx_during_tx" and
guard the whole else-if branch, which keeps the option chain valid in
every macro combination.  On kernels without the constant the option
is rejected as unsupported instead of breaking the build.

On Freetz this hits exactly the x86 cable boxes (6490, 6590) whose
kernels are 2.6.39.x: new enough for TIOCSRS485, old enough to lack
the constant.  All other targets either predate TIOCSRS485 (the whole
RS485 block is compiled out) or have the constant (kernel >= 3.7).

--- a/lib/os_osops.c
+++ b/lib/os_osops.c
@@ -3017,8 +3017,10 @@
 	    t->rs485.flags |= SER_RS485_RTS_ON_SEND;
 	} else if (strcmp(argv[i], "rts_after_send") == 0) {
 	    t->rs485.flags |= SER_RS485_RTS_AFTER_SEND;
+#ifdef SER_RS485_RX_DURING_TX
 	} else if (strcmp(argv[i], "rx_during_tx") == 0) {
 	    t->rs485.flags |= SER_RS485_RX_DURING_TX;
+#endif
 #ifdef SER_RS485_TERMINATE_BUS
 	} else if (strcmp(argv[i], "terminate_bus") == 0) {
 	    t->rs485.flags |= SER_RS485_TERMINATE_BUS;
